00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.440
I know I'm preaching to the choir here.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.600
Everybody's AI native.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.520
That's why we're at the con here.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.840
But the way we build software

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.880
has changed pretty significantly
in the last six months.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.960
I would say in December,
with the introduction of GPT 5.2,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.560
Opus 4.5, we really reach
singularity levels of software engineering

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.120
and code production being something
that these tools do insanely well.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.960
And this is a level of disruption
that I think we have typically only seen

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.040
once every decade here.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.080
The last one that I can think of
is probably like

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.160
the existence of the cloud
as a tool to accelerate ourselves.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.120
And with that sort of like cadence
of disruptive innovation,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.520
we have had a lot of time
to internalize changes

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.520
to our workflows
and the way we go about building.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.760
But here

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.720
the technology keeps

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.680
changing so rapidly
with every point release of these models

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.120
where I find myself
very often having to reevaluate

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.040
my priors of what even is possible
to achieve with these tools.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.360
And I think if you're not in the habit

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.000
of kind of completely retooling your stack
and the way you work with every point

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.840
release of the model,
you are in a way missing out on

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.840
what it is
that you can achieve with these tools.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.320
The reasons that

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.560
the way we have built
software has changed and continues

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.760
changing at an increasingly rapid pace
is because we have kind of upended

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.880
some of the core axioms
of what it means to build software.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.000
Right now, I'm telling you,
the models are good enough in order to do

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.840
significant parts of the software
engineering lifecycle not just writing

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.760
code, but debugging, triaging,
responding to customers, planning,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.600
scheduling work, all these other bits
that are outside of the core.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.880
Would you say production
function of a software engineer?

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.120
A lot of the way we have tooled

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.120
teams and organizations
and roadmaps have been built around

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.880
this idea that the production of code
is this very, very expensive thing

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.760
that is going to dominate most
of our headcount resources and is slow.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.120
And in this world where we can give
a prompt to a coding agent

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.640
and get a PR or six out of it,
that constraint is no longer true.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.640
And we kind of have

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.760
these teams
who were doing the bulk of the production

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.760
for software that need to figure out ways
to increase their leverage

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.040
by delegating increasing parts
of that responsibility to these machines.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.000
So for all the software engineers
and engineering managers and product

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.560
managers and designers

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.120
who are trying to incorporate
this technology into your work,

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.960
all of your goals are to be how to unblock
your execution team, these coding agents

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.280
from being able to make your ideas,
your vision, and your products a reality.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.520
So having just told

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.080
everybody here
that the core constraints on software

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:31.280
engineering no longer apply,
what are those core constraints, right.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.080
We have kind of a new set of problems
to contend with, using agents

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.640
in order to produce our software.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:41.800
And to me, these three things
are the foundational limits that remain

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.520
in a world where we are as a team
of humans and agents producing software.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.360
Human time is the fundamentally scarce
resource that we have.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:56.720
You know, I know I max out probably
at three concurrent sessions on my laptop.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.000
If I want to be more parallel
and have higher throughput,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.080
I must find ways to remove my own
synchronous attention from the process.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.560
Human and model attention.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.280
Are these foundational limits right?

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.280
In the architecture
of these llms, attention must sum to one

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.240
thrashing the agents
by having them do more and more work

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.520
with conflicting and overbearing
requirements in the course of a task

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.840
is something that is always going
to degrade performance less

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.680
and less over time, but it is one of those
core limits of the models.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.960
So we need to retool the way we work
in order to be more parallel.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.560
Fork off a bunch more tasks,
be willing to accept smaller or larger

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.080
or many more press in order
to let the agents explore what it means to

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.920
do the job that we need them to do.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:47.920
And finally, you all probably deeply
live this model context window.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.200
Things that get bigger over time.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.200
Still a scarce resource,
something we need to protect.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.960
I will say, in my own experience
with the GPT series of models,

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.480
auto compaction is fantastic.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.760
I never think about a context window
anymore.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.840
I can let a task go for six, 12, 36 hours
and still get good results,

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.560
but the context window
being obliterated and rebuilt

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:13.080
over the course of these auto completions
is something you must contend with.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:16.680
And there are ways
that we structure the context.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:17.600
We give the model, or

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.960
continually resurface
context to the model to deal with this

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.400
constraint, that context windows
are continually being emptied and filled.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:27.440
Okay, so

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.720
we've got these agents that we hope
can produce more and more of our software,

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:32.640
that we can remove

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.680
humans more and more from the loop
in order to produce more code,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.840
more features,
solve more user needs, address

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.880
more critical user journeys
with higher quality and fidelity.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.600
How do we make sure that we as a team
with our agents, do a good job?

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.600
And I think

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.480
a new ish thing here is
we have to actually articulate that.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.080
We have to write it down.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.400
I know, like it used to be the case.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.160
Oh, we'll have people into the office.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.120
We'll have meetings through osmosis.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.640
People will understand what it means

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.560
for us as a team to write high quality
software to work effectively together.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.080
And agents
just do not have that capability.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.560
They don't have presence in our stand up.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.520
They don't have this durable memory
that accumulates context and battle

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:19.800
scars over time.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.600
So we have to find ways
to make all these nonfunctional

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.080
requirements of writing good software
legible to the agent.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.760
And as an LM, the thing that it craves,
the thing that drives it is text.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.800
So figuring out ways
to take the definition of what it means

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.760
to do a good job and write it down is a.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.440
Net new function
for a software engineering team in 2026,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.920
but it's
not enough to just write things down.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.560
We need to make sure that this text is
a thing

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:55.480
that the agent will look at,
because it doesn't do much to say.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.080
You will write reliable network code
by making sure that retries and timeouts

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.840
are consistently applied
if that text never makes it to the agent.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:07.120
So figuring out ways
that not only we can write things down,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.800
but also have them pulled into context
at the right time

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.200
in ways that don't thrash the agent
and still lead it to be creative.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.520
And reason,
which are the power of these models, is

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.520
the important thing.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.360
So to kind of take a step back
and look at some

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.560
in the small instances of context
and what it means to kind of like

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:35.000
think systematically and close
loops for the models and for your team.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:36.840
If I were

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.240
onboarding a new engineer to my team
and we were,

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.560
I was reviewing some react code
that they had written.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.360
And I knew for this
particular set of components

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.360
we use suspense because that leads
to better performance in the front end.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.720
I would be able to give that feedback
once to the human,

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.760
and they would incorporate it
into their mental model of the code

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.240
base, what it means for these
different screens to relate to each other.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.720
Well, and I would largely solve
that problem going forward by empowering

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:11.120
my teammate to know more
about what it means to do a good job.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.440
But I can't really do that
with an agent in the same way.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.440
So I kind of have to step back,
give that review

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.440
feedback on an agent produced PR,
and then myself

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:26.640
figure out a way to make these mistakes
statically impossible going forward.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.040
And it might be the case that I'm
looking at all those review comments.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:34.840
Seeing this is missing context,
that the agent was not able

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:38.760
to pull in at the right time to know that
the code that it wrote was misaligned.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.920
How do I figure out where I can write it
down, what links I can have fail?

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.000
What tests need to exist,
whether or not I can empower a reviewer

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.640
agent to look at all the proposed diffs
through the lens of these guardrails

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.560
to make it so that this feedback
is actually durably encoded

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:59.960
as a static guardrail
that we apply to every PR going forward.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:03.600
It's not enough to do point in time
fixes with these models.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.600
We want to make every mistake
something that is just not possible.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.280
I never want to give the
same review feedback twice,

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:12.320
and this

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.400
is really the core of what harness
engineering is.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.680
Harness engineering is making
context around what it means to do

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.760
a good job, legible and then just in time,
surface to the agent

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.360
over the course of its trajectories
in order to steer and refine its output,

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.600
to make sure that every PR
we get adheres to the golden thread

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.600
of what we consider
to be acceptable, high quality aligned

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.640
software.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.840
It's kind of funny
when you work with these agents

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:50.120
that what I would normally consider
to be like good practice

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:51.760
around DevOps and shifting left

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.600
as far as possible in order to make things
cheaper earlier in the process.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.720
I don't do that at all
when working with agents.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:03.840
In fact, I try and put my interventions
as far right in the process as I can

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:08.280
in order to minimize my own synchronous
time having to engage with these issues.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:13.360
For example, if I'm working on a PR
and I realize I get a bad result,

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.760
it might just be the case
that I'll trash it, change my prompt,

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.320
and probably get something good out of it.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:21.120
But that's not really a durable thing.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:22.080
It's not reliable.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.520
I don't socialize
those improvements to my team,

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:28.920
so it's sort of the next level of shifting
that left is to write it down.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.920
And if writing it down is not enough,

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:35.040
writing down
and then empowering or a view agent to

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.920
judge every diff is another way
I can shift that left,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.280
and then I can shift it left further
into statically verifiable links

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:46.400
and guardrails and tests and on and on
and on earlier in the process.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:52.520
And we think about this as needing
to surface to the agent,

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.760
all those sets
of nonfunctional requirements.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.800
It is not the case that these agents don't
know how to write high quality software.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:01.960
They absolutely do.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:07.240
But as artifacts of their training,
they have seen every possible

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:11.400
permutation of every possible choice
that goes into producing software.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.640
And it's up to us to prune latent space

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.160
to tell it which choices we want to make.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.560
If I am using these things

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.400
to prototype a new data science model
in a Jupyter notebook,

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.200
I have a very different set of choices
I make in the production of those diffs

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.400
than I do
if I am working on adding a new index type

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.400
to a database,
you're just fundamentally different tasks.

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:41.160
So it's up to us, as owners of our code
bases, to make legible

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.280
the sets of decisions
that we make in order to produce our code,

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:48.400
what it means for something
to be a prototype versus production

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.160
feature that requires a stage
rollout with a B test and feature flags.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:57.800
And if we write this down
and give the agent some tools to reason

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:02.040
about what type of changes being made
to find, run books that are appropriate

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:07.120
to refining its output
over the course of its PR and epics.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:12.320
We can give it bounds and context,
but still give it the space to reason.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.880
Be creative and cook.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:18.080
One maybe

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:22.160
non-obvious thing is that
because the agents crave text,

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:27.400
every bit of text that we feed
them is in some sense prompting.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:29.960
It's going to inform
what tokens get predicted,

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.080
which means it's going to inform the code
and the diffs that we produce.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.280
This means all the code
in the repository of itself,

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.760
outside of the documentation
knowledge base is also prompts.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:43.640
So if we think about aligning

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:48.200
the code base or unifying it
all on the same patterns, we kind of limit

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:52.120
the amount of attention
the model needs in order to do a good job.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:56.120
If I am able to standardize
on across my entire stack.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.120
For example,
when the model thinks observable,

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:03.200
it's able to translate context
that it sees in one part of the repository

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.240
over to something 
halfway across the code base

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.160
without any loss of quality
or intelligence.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.000
But if I have six observable
stacks in the code base,

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:12.760
the model is going to have

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:16.680
to spend a lot more time
figuring out which one do I use here?

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:18.520
Is this migrated or not?

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.680
What is canonically good?

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.400
So over the course of the PR, there's

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:28.960
sort of three phases I think about
when we're talking about context delivery.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:34.160
And because we are curating the code base
in order to make it efficient

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.160
to deliver context to these agents,
we also want to encode that

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.200
in the operating loop, we give the model.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.200
To me, the most important thing
that ends up in that agent

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:46.600
file is a numbered set of steps
that we expect the model

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.200
to go through over every rollout
that we do over every session,

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:54.280
we first want it to ground itself
in the documentation

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.280
knowledge base in the ticket
that is proposed.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.480
We want it to spider through our history
of address and design docs,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:05.320
to figure out how this might impact
other features of our code base.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.440
We want it to look at the critical user
journeys to inform itself around what

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:14.120
screens and user surfaces are impacted,
so it can keep the QA plan in mind.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.120
Over the course of its execution.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:19.120
We expect some amount of slowness
during this process,

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.720
because we want to page
in all the context around

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.800
what it means for this feature
to slot in globally.

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:31.520
Then there's sort of a messy middle
part of the run where the agent is writing

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.640
code, running test,
exploring the code base, and for that,

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:41.320
we exploit the fact that these agents
are going to call a bunch of tools,

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:45.240
run a bunch of tests in order to use them
to just in time, prompt

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.360
inject the agent
to steer its output back to baseline.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.560
The tests we write, the lists

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:55.280
we write for agents are very different
than the ones that we write for humans.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:00.600
They by default recognize
that agents are going to truncate

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.240
tool call outputs, that they respond
really well to descriptive error messages

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:07.240
that point them to run books
for remediation steps.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.720
And we are willing
to have very many of these things

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:12.920
that are kind of fiddly to write.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:13.960
And I wouldn't normally think

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:17.920
about to go back to this sort of network
code example.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.880
I am sure all of you have been paged
at some point in your careers

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:25.040
around an outage
that boiled down to a missing

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:28.720
timeout and a retry on a cross service
network call, and

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.760
the collective amount of engineering time

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:34.720
that has been spent on this very common
failure mode is astounding.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.200
But still today,

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:40.920
like there's there's there's no code
that asserts that we pass retries around.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.120
There's no ES lint plugin
that I can slot into my code

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.680
base that's going to do this for me.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.880
But because the production of code
is very, very cheap now,

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:53.840
we can absolutely vibe a set of guardrails
into place with 100% code

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.760
coverage and exhaustive table
driven tests, and migrate the code base

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.920
all in one go and just in time surface
this failure to the model

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:05.680
every time it writes another fetch call
and never have to worry about this again.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:10.520
And because we don't have to pollute
context window up front,

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:13.840
and we can exploit the fact
that a tool called output is going to be

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.160
given less weight
during an auto compaction.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.840
We just in time, correct
the model and still let it go off

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:24.080
and do the complex work
that we wanted to in our original prompt.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:27.880
And then sort of after the run,
we have a much easier

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:30.480
task of determining
whether or not the code, the diff,

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:34.320
the artifact is aligned because
it's a static thing, and we have static

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.160
sets of guardrails
and can use very, very many LM

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.160
as judge to look at the code,

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:45.160
operationalize it
with a set or three of static guardrails.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.920
This is what it means
to write reliable code.

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.520
This is what it means to write performant
react and make a determination.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:53.840
Is this good or bad?

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.600
And if it's bad, why is it bad?

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.960
Because the LMS crave text.

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:03.320
These LMS judges can collaborate
with the implementation agent over that

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:06.760
PR thread, give more text
back to the implementation agent,

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.680
and further realign
the proposed diff back to baseline.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:13.880
So we've got agents

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:17.680
kind of as this map
that shows where the context is during

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.040
what types of work
the model might want to look at that text,

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:24.840
but otherwise not being prescriptive
around any of the guardrails.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.160
We don't want to jam
a ton of rules in here,

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.880
because we're going to chop up
latent space too much.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:36.400
We're going to make it difficult for
the model to spider through the code base.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.160
With creativity.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:42.160
I find it very, very useful from this

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:45.720
to point to a curated set of review

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.240
personas that are essentially bulleted
list of guardrails.

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:52.960
And I find this really, really neat
for an interfacing with the other humans

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:57.000
on the team perspective,
because it is so cheap

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.560
as a team to have a slack conversation
in a thread around

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:02.680
what it means to

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:07.160
fix that performance regression,
and then mention the agent in it to say,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:11.840
you want all of this and put up a PR that
adds it to our static set of guardrails.

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:12.760
So cheap.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:16.440
In order to continually refine and improve
the output of our agents in that way.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.080
I also think it's really neat

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:23.360
to take that same sort of pattern
and apply it toward

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:26.960
documenting what your product
features are, or what the critical user

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:32.000
journeys are, or why your apps even exist,
what user problems they solve.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.200
All this context
that we can give the agent helps

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.760
ground it
in what we are trying to do and why.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.040
How our team thinks about working.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.640
Because all of this is going to produce
more and more aligned output.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:48.320
In that messy middle.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:51.840
We can kind of use tests on the AST,

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:57.000
tests on the structure of the files
on disk, really blunt

00:20:57.040 --> 00:21:01.320
hammers around file line counts,
or whether or not snapshot tests exist.

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:04.400
These very,
very coarse grained tools, in order

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.400
to make the model do what we know is good,

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.240
just requiring that every react

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.240
component in our codebase
has a snapshot test

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.960
that gives 100% branch coverage,

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.040
means that the model

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:21.080
is naturally decomposing these things
and making them pure where possible,

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:25.360
and not doing prop drilling and putting
hooks close to where the data is used,

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.040
because that makes it easier for it
to fill

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.640
the requirement
that there must be snapshot tests.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.920
And we can do this.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.800
We can assert this because it's free
to produce the code that spiders through

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:40.080
disk, and matches up the snapshot test
to the underlying component.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.120
Another failure mode that I hear folks
talk about a bunch

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:50.720
is that these agents are doing
type shaped probing all the time.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.080
I end up with these anys or unknowns
all over the code

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:57.360
base, and the way I've approached
it is to just statically disallow

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.840
any function that has a type
of any or unknown, unless it's

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:05.320
pausing input in a root handler
or from the database.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.720
Other than that, with ES lint,
we just ban the existence of that type.

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:14.160
We require the code base to be 100% typed,
which means all this bad

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:17.760
behavior and weird type probing
just kind of falls out.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.200
Because we require 100% code coverage.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:24.000
These functions cannot possibly
be exercised because the unknown types can

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.960
exist, and we get more aligned code,
more aligned code that I would consider

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:32.960
acceptable, high quality, maintainable,
and all these other sorts of properties.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:35.080
And having these

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:39.560
failing checks tell the agent
why they failed, and what to do instead

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.600
means that it's able to self heal.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.280
Ultimately, as we move into

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:50.400
that third phase of review and merge,
we want to treat the model

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:54.160
as if it's another member of the team
and it needs to convince me to merge

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.280
its code.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:00.600
I'm not shoulder surfing
any of my teammates in VS code or vim

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.960
when they put up a PR,
and they attest that they tested the code,

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.720
I take their word, you know?

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:09.840
And if I am unsure, I'll ask them to
show me the logs from the staging deploy

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:14.880
or to post a screenshot of them
exercising the feature in the app.

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:18.840
And we can require these agents
to do the same thing.

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:22.320
It says a lot easier these days.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:25.600
Now that we have things like computer
use and browser use,

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:28.400
the Codex app is fantastic.
Highly recommended.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:32.840
But even without that,
you know, vibing yourself up on X corks,

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:37.440
connected headless display
in a Docker container and wiring up

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:41.520
FFM peg to that stream to record
a reproduction video is within reach.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.120
Because I don't care about how gross
this code is, and Codex is able to sling

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:49.280
ffmpeg better than anybody
in this room, probably.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.800
On the back half of things

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:58.440
where we are looking for ways
to accept the diff.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.600
I'm treating it again like I would
my human teammates benefit of the doubt

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:03.880
biased toward merge.

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:07.160
What are the P2 and above things
that would be necessary for me

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:08.720
to accept this code?

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:12.200
Use the reviewer agents,
which is really just a matrix CI job

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:16.840
that points out a bunch of markdown files
to judge this thing, drive

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:19.880
these remediation to completion,

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:23.280
get the coding agent to pick them up,

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:26.720
implement it, get the reviewers
to be happy, and off we go.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:31.040
And this sort of process with me
observing along the way

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:34.040
of which review feedback
is regularly getting surfaced.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:36.600
Why is it making it
to this part of the pipeline?

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:37.680
Maybe I need to use

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.680
that as a signal that I need to shift
some of these guardrails left,

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:44.120
and then I can spend my time
and the reviewer agents can spend

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.120
their time on more bespoke or

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:50.480
more complex changes
that we need them to look at.

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:53.880
Another thing

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:58.320
that you should be thinking about doing
as a team is how to systematize capturing

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:00.000
all of this human feedback,

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.680
every review comment, every time you have
had to interrupt the agent,

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:10.240
every agent intervention, every failed
build, every exception in production.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.480
All of these, in some sense, are signals
that context was missing

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.240
to the implementation agent,
that it did not consider the full end

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.240
to end consequences
of the code that it wrote,

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.560
and whether or not
it would be successfully deployed.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:26.560
And what we are trying to do,
which I expect you'll learn

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:30.640
about in the next talk, is slurp
all this data up and dream over it

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:35.200
every night, pointing a bunch of sub
agents at it, trying to distill

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.320
whether or not

00:25:36.320 --> 00:25:38.000
there's anything that humans can do better

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.320
in their prompting,
whether there's missing guardrails

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.320
that should exist in the code

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:45.000
base that disallow this behavior,
and how we can get to a world where

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:50.040
we're more and more headless,
less human, interrupt dependent, and able

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.920
to trust the agent to do more and more
complex things helplessly.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:57.440
I think

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.440
vibe coding is a big part of what it takes
to be successful here,

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.480
because there's a ton of guardrails that
only affect my local development process.

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:07.880
This code can be gross,
but it brings into possibility

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:11.440
this idea that I don't need

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.520
to care about some parts
of the software production function.

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:18.560
This lets me operate like a group tech
lead or an org lead, where

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:23.440
I don't have visibility into every single
engineers activity on the keyboard.

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:27.760
But the thing I care about
are invariants, interfaces, whether or not

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:29.280
the components that they're producing

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.280
do what they say
on the tin with high reliability.

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:36.200
And with that, I'll just leave it with
y'all can go build things.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.840
These tools are fantastic.
Go get after it.

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.440
I'll take some questions now.

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.360
Thank you so much.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:46.680
Thank you.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.680
Pao, that hand went up really fast.

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.320
Hold on one second.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.320
I want to grab that one.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:55.360
Hello.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.760
Great talk.

00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:00.600
You mentioned earlier in your talk
that you find

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:04.840
that you don't need to shift
left as much as before.

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:05.960
You stay more. Right.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:11.440
And I'm curious about that,
because isn't it better for agents

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:17.400
to see something in a lint rule rather
than as review feedback, for example?

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:23.120
Like what do you mean by 
staying more right and not shifting left?

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:27.400
So I think once you kind of put
these structures in place to surface

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:32.560
these requirements to the models
at the right time, it becomes pretty easy

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:36.040
to rely on them for the most part
to auto discover this stuff.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:40.240
It is very often the case that our agents

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:43.920
paints a picture of which guardrail files
are relevant,

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.720
for which categories of changes backend
working on the design system,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:51.080
these sorts of things
where the models will just naturally page

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:54.560
those sets of persona
oriented guardrails into into context,

00:27:54.600 --> 00:28:00.160
which means I very often just don't see
patterns of misbehavior in that way.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:03.240
Only if, for example,

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:09.280
guardrails are commonly required
over tasks that span 15 context windows,

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:13.200
and by then the context in those guardrail
files has been auto compacted away,

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:16.680
then that's the sort of thing
that I would use as a signal that, okay,

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:19.960
this is the thing that I need to shift
shift left further on.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:24.440
But I do recognize here
that it is sort of predicated

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:29.560
on making sure that those auto discovery
functions are things that are reliable.

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:34.640
I probably got time for one more here.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:40.360
Is there a practical implementation
of those of the harness

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:45.440
you've just mentioned, in terms of 
some end to end implementation

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:50.280
of those capabilities during,
before, during, and after?

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:54.640
I have started to bring some of these
techniques to my open source work.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:29:00.400
I used to long ago build a Ruby
interpreter in Russ called Artichoke.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:04.240
There's a bunch of crates out of
that work that I still actively maintain.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:07.840
Probably the most interesting
one for you to take a peek

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:11.240
at is rand-mt — artichoke-rand-mt.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.920
It's a sort of Mersenne Twister
implementation.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:19.160
Been doing a lot of fun stuff
exploiting automations in the Codex app

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:20.440
to basically take my hands

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:23.440
off the wheel for a ton of the maintenance
tasks of this OSS work.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:25.840
I haven't quite gotten
to putting those review agents in

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.120
place yet, but it's coming.

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:31.760
Any final questions?

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:32.480
Nope.

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:35.120
Okay, big round of applause for Ryan.
Thank you so much.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:38.720
Thank you everyone. Thank you.

